CHDIR(2)                       P32W Unix                        CHDIR(2)

NAME
       chdir - change the current directory

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       int chdir(const char *path);

DESCRIPTION
       chdir makes path the current directory of the calling
       process: the starting point for path names that do not
       begin with /. The caller needs search (x) permission on it.
       The current directory is inherited by fork(2) and kept
       across exec(2).

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       ENOENT  path does not exist.
       ENOTDIR path, or a component of its prefix, is not a
               directory.
       EACCES  Search permission is denied on path or on a
               directory on the way.
       EFAULT  path is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               path is 256 characters or more, or a component is
               longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       sh(1), pwd(1), stat(2)

BUGS
       There is no chroot and no fchdir.
